##7. Deadline Recovery Plan
Project	Recovery action	Trade-off / decision
Taxi Booking	Review critical path, prioritize minimum agreed release scope, remove blockers, and provide phased delivery options.	Defer lower-priority features only with client approval; do not reduce quality controls.
Restaurant POS	Complete integration readiness review, reserve QA windows, triage defects by severity, and retest fixes.	Release date may need adjustment if critical defects remain open.
E-Commerce	Confirm API contract and backend readiness; agree interface changes before integration.	Use approved mocks only to unblock parallel work; final integration requires real services.
Employee Management	Hold client clarification and obtain written acceptance criteria.	Do not treat unclear requirements as accepted scope.


##8. Escalation Thresholds
Trigger	Escalation level	Required response
Task forecast to miss agreed date by 1 working day	Project Lead	Confirm blocker, owner and recovery action.
Milestone forecast to slip by more than 2 working days	Project Manager	Review impact, re-sequence work and notify stakeholders.
Critical-path task blocked for more than 1 working day	Immediate Project Manager escalation	Assign decision owner and unblock or approve recovery option.
Resource allocation exceeds 40 hours per person per week	Project Manager / Resource Lead	Rebalance scope or resources; do not approve over-capacity allocation.
Critical defect remains open near release	QA Lead and Project Manager	Hold release decision until agreed release criteria are met.
Client requests work outside agreed scope	Project Manager / Change Control	Log change request and assess scope, time, cost and capacity.
Taxi Booking recovery cannot protect agreed date	Management escalation	Present phased scope, revised date, or additional approved capacity options.


Thresholds are proposed controls for this assessment and should be aligned with company policy and client agreements.
9. Weekly Monitoring Routine
1. Update task status, completion, actual hours, and remaining estimate.
2. Confirm blockers and dependency owners.
3. Review upcoming milestones and forecast variance.
4. Check individual resource allocations against weekly capacity.
5. Review risks, issues, change requests and decisions.
6. Publish concise client updates for material changes.
7. Escalate according to the agreed thresholds.

##10. Day 3 Conclusion
The Day 3 plan provides a single view of project progress, remaining effort, milestone sequence, dependencies, recovery actions, and escalation rules. Taxi Booking remains the highest-risk project and requires immediate scope and critical-path review. Restaurant POS needs coordinated integration and QA; E-Commerce needs backend/API readiness; and Employee Management needs approved acceptance criteria.

The proposed estimates and relative schedule windows must be validated before they are treated as a delivery baseline. The next step is to apply the Day 4 incident scenarios, update the tracker, and record the impact of each decision.
